--
-- SOURCE. A plain-text rendering of the Act from legislation.gov.uk (Open Government Licence v3.0) made by a converter script; it is
-- not the official text and nothing here is "verified against the official text". Line numbers are of ukpga-2006-46.txt.
-- TARGET. The stable `l4` (main) 1.6.110. No section GIVEN, no REFUSE, no sets, no TYPICALLY, no YMD.
--
-- READING A RESULT (rule of 2026-10-29, assumed, not ruled by a lawyer): JUST TRUE = the text says the case is within the provision;
-- JUST FALSE = the text says no in its own words ("only if", "if" read as the text's no, a flat negative); NOTHING = the text does not
-- decide (a "may" grant limited by its own wording, a list not closed, silent, or the answer comes from another section).
--
-- WHAT THE TEXT LEAVES TO OTHER SECTIONS. Whether an address is "the company's registered office" is s 86 (coa2): the input
-- `s 86 the company has its registered office at the address` is a MAYBE BOOLEAN, so a NOTHING from s 86 stays NOTHING (PLAN.md section 4).
-- Whether a provision of the Companies Acts "authorises" a document to be sent to an address, or deems an agreement or a specification, is
-- the provision that does so; "hard copy", "electronic form" and "electronic means" are s 1168 (the keel). Each of those is a plain input
-- below: a fact the person can state, or the answer of the other section.

§ `Schedule 4 -- Documents and information sent or supplied to a company`

-- The three forms the Schedule distinguishes. The core's `Form of document` has only hard copy and electronic form; Part 4 deals with
-- a form that is neither (NEEDS-CORE.md).
DECLARE `sch 4 form of the document or information` IS ONE OF
    `sch 4 hard copy form`
    `sch 4 electronic form`
    `sch 4 neither hard copy form nor electronic form`

-- How the document or information goes: by hand, by post, by electronic means (e-mail, fax), or some other way.
DECLARE `sch 4 means of sending or supplying` IS ONE OF
    `sch 4 by hand`
    `sch 4 by post`
    `sch 4 by electronic means`
    `sch 4 by some other means`

§§ `Part 1 -- Introduction`

-- src:19397 | 1 (1) This Schedule applies to documents or information sent or supplied to a company.
-- src:19398 | (2) It does not apply to documents or information sent or supplied by another company (see section 1144(3) and Schedule 5).
@ref sch 4 para 1 Application of Schedule (src:19397-19398)
GIVEN `the document or information is sent or supplied to a company` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the document or information is sent or supplied by another company` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 4 para 1 this Schedule applies to the document or information` MEANS
    IF `the document or information is sent or supplied by another company`
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "It does not apply to documents or information sent or supplied by another company (see section 1144(3) and Schedule 5)"
    ELSE IF `the document or information is sent or supplied to a company`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "This Schedule applies to documents or information sent or supplied to a company"

§§ `Part 2 -- Communications in hard copy form`

-- Para 3(2): "sends by post" for this Schedule is posting a prepaid envelope.
-- src:19404 | (2) For the purposes of this Schedule, a person sends a document or information by post if he posts a prepaid envelope containing the document or information.
@ref sch 4 para 3(2) Posting a prepaid envelope (src:19404)
GIVEN `the person posts a prepaid envelope containing the document or information` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 4 para 3(2) the person sends the document or information by post` MEANS
